Sure, I can explain it to you, but I need to warn you, it's a bit of a mouthful.

A null pointer exception is a runtime exception that occurs when you try to access or manipulate a null (or non-existent) object in your code.

A null pointer is an object that has no value or no reference to any object. It's like a blank canvas where you can't draw anything.

Here's an example in Java:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str = null;
        System.out.println(str.length()); // This will throw a NullPointerException
    }
}
```

In this example, `str` is a null reference because it's not assigned a value. When you try to access `str.length()`, you're trying to access a non-existent object.

To avoid this, you should always check if a variable is null before trying to access its properties or methods:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str = null;
        if (str != null) {
            System.out.println(str.length()); // This will work
        } else {
            System.out.println("String is null");
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, the `if` statement checks if `str` is not null before trying to access its properties. If it's null, the code prints "String is null".

Remember, null pointer exceptions can be tricky to catch, so it's always a good idea to write robust code that handles these exceptions.
